import pf.Html
import ../../Demos/Echo
import ../../Demos/Shout
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import pf.Endpoint
import ../../Generated/Api
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Note
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../Ui/Demo
import ../../Ui/Trace
import ../../Ui/Who
import ../../Ui/Versus

## Talking to the server: endpoints, read where a page renders, sent from handlers, and the events
## one streams.
meta = {
	title: "Talking to the server",
	description: "rocx endpoints, a route's +server.roc: read as plain values where a page renders, answered by the server as it renders and carried in the page; sent from handlers; and the events one streams.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "reads", label: "A page's data: Endpoint.get!" },
		{ id: "how", label: "From the server's render to a click" },
		{ id: "problems", label: "When it goes wrong" },
		{ id: "user", label: "Who is asking" },
		{ id: "endpoints", label: "Endpoints: +server.roc" },
		{ id: "events", label: "Events, as they happen" },
		{ id: "forms", label: "Forms: actions and submissions" },
		{ id: "build", label: "What rocx build does" },
		{ id: "server-functions", label: "server!, deprecated" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Limits, for now" },
	],
}

## The endpoints, as they answered: this path's own endpoint (`GET /calls`, for JSON), which the
## server answers as it renders the page.
asked! : {} => { asked : List({ method : Str, path : Str, body : Str, status : U16, answer : Str }), shouted : Str }
asked! = |{}| Endpoint.get!(Api.get_calls({}))

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Talking to the server"
				title="One definition, the endpoint's"
			>
				A route's <code>+server.roc</code> is an endpoint: a method at a URL, typed at both ends. A
				component reads a GET endpoint as a plain value. The server answers it as it renders the page,
				and the page carries the answer, so the browser asks nothing to wake up. Handlers send endpoints
				the rest, and listen to the events one streams. Anything else that asks over HTTP (another app,
				<code>curl</code>) gets the same answer, behind the same middleware.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="reads">A page's data: Endpoint.get!</Heading>
			<p>
				An endpoint's <code>get!</code> answers with its data, and <code>Endpoint.get!</code> reads it where a
				component renders, as a plain value: in a memo, or straight in the markup. Here, an endpoint that says
				a message back; a signal a click writes; a memo that reads the endpoint for each message there is; and
				another that finds the answer to the one picked.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_echo.file} source={Sources.endpoint_echo.rocx} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.calls_component.file}, in part"} source={Sources.calls_component.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo inspect="echo">
			<div data-demo="echo" data-flash="yes"><Echo /></div>
			<NoHydration>
				<Note>
					This component, live. Every answer came with the page: the server read the endpoint for each
					message as it rendered, so a click asks nothing, and works on a copy of this site that is only
					files.
				</Note>
			</NoHydration>
		</Demo>

		<NoHydration>
			<Heading id="how">From the server's render to a click</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The component runs, as the server renders the page. The memo
					reads <code>{"GET /api/echo/{message}"}</code> for each message, and the server answers it
					in-process: through the endpoint's route, as it would answer the browser, with
					<code>accept: application/json</code> and the page's cookies and headers. The same middleware,
					the same answer, and nothing to wait for. A URL read twice is asked once.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The page goes out with the picked message's answer in
					the <code>{"<output>"}</code>, and every answer in a script at its end, each under its URL
					(<code>GET /api/echo/hello</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> Hydrating, the same component runs. The memo reads the same
					URLs, finds their answers in the page, and asks nothing; the server's nodes are kept. This site's
					tests hydrate every page strictly: a page that asks the server for anything it didn't carry fails
					them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A click: <code>{"on:click={|| set_draft!(message!())}"}</code>
					writes the signal, the memo that reads it runs again and finds that message's answer among the
					others, and the one text that reads it is patched. Nothing is asked: every answer is a value
					already.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A read whose answer isn't in the page (its URL built from a
					signal an <code>{"<input>"}</code> writes, say), or one read again with
					<code>refresh!(memo!)</code>, is a GET of the endpoint's own URL, for JSON. Until the answer is
					in, the memo keeps its last value, so what is shown stays; <code>{"<Loading>"}</code> only waits for
					first values. Answers may come back in any order: the memo only takes the one for what it asked
					last.
				</li>
			</Trace>
			<p>
				What an endpoint answers is JSON, derived by Roc from the handler's types, and
				<code>Generated/Api.roc</code> carries those types to the component: reading it as anything else
				doesn't compile. A page's own data is best at the page's own URL. <code>routes/members/+server.roc</code>
				answers <code>GET /members</code> for JSON, and the page at <code>/members</code> reads it, behind
				the same middleware. The page's query goes with it when it matters:
				<code>{"Endpoint.with_query(Api.get_board({}), Router.location!({}).query)"}</code> reads
				<code>/?weeks=1</code> for the page at <code>/?weeks=1</code>, and the handler reads
				<code>request.query</code>. A layout, which many pages share, reads an endpoint at a path of its own:
				this site's footer reads <code>GET /api/facts</code>.
			</p>
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.members_read.file}, in part"} source={Sources.members_read.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.endpoint_members.file} source={Sources.endpoint_members.rocx} />
			</Versus>

			<Heading id="problems">When it goes wrong</Heading>
			<p>
				An answer that isn't 200 fails what read it: the memo, the hole. The nearest
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> shows its fallback, given why, and a handler that reads again. Why is one of
				<code>Server.Problem</code>: no answer (<code>HttpErr</code>), a refusal with its status
				(<code>Refused</code>: 404 for a path the server doesn't have, or what the endpoint or its middleware
				refused with), or an answer of another type (<code>BadAnswer</code>, from a server of another build).
				<code>Endpoint.read!</code> reads the same answer with its refusal as a value
				(<code>Err(Refused(404, why))</code>), for a component that shows it itself. A refusal the server met
				as it rendered goes in the page too, so the browser fails where the server did, without asking.
			</p>
			<p>
				A read its middleware answers with a redirect (a signed-out visitor sent to sign in) makes the whole
				page that redirect, as SolidStart answers one thrown while it renders. A layout's reads answer every
				page's visitors: nothing is above the root layout to catch a failure there, so it puts an
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> around the holes that read, or the page is a 500.
			</p>

			<Heading id="user">Who is asking</Heading>
			<p>
				The GET the server makes as it renders carries the page's cookies and headers. So a handler reads who
				is asking from the request, whether the browser sent it or the server made it, and never takes the
				browser's word for it. The bag on the <a href={"${Paths.components}#islands"}>components page</a> says
				whose bag it is this way:
			</p>
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.bag_reader.file}, in part"} source={Sources.bag_reader.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.endpoint_reader.file} source={Sources.endpoint_reader.rocx} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				Its route runs behind middleware: the app's default, around every request, and the route file's own
				<code>middleware</code>, as its page has. What the middleware handed on (who is signed in, say) is the
				handler's <code>context</code>: <code>{"get! = |{ context, .. }| Ok({ member: context.member })"}</code>.
				Its body may use what only the server has: <code>Db</code>, <code>Job</code>, <code>Email</code>
				(<a href={Paths.platform_postgres}>Postgres</a>). The browser's copy of <code>Api</code> holds the
				endpoint's method and URL, and nothing it does.
			</p>

			<Heading id="endpoints">Endpoints: +server.roc</Heading>
			<p>
				A route's <code>+server.roc</code> (plain Roc; <code>+server.rocx</code> too, if it has markup)
				answers HTTP methods at its path: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>,
				<code>patch!</code> and <code>delete!</code>. Each is given the request, the path's params, what its
				middleware handed on and, with a body, the body, read as JSON. It answers <code>Ok(result)</code>,
				sent as JSON, or a refusal, sent as its status (<code>Endpoint.Refusal</code>:
				<code>BadRequest(why)</code> is a 400).
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_shout.file} source={Sources.endpoint_shout.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_shout_word.file} source={Sources.endpoint_shout_word.rocx} />
			<p>Over HTTP, anything may ask them. What they answered, asked by this page's own endpoint as the page rendered:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table class="http">
					<thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Body</th><th>Status</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						{...asked!({}).asked.map(|{ method, path, body, status, answer }|
							<tr>
								<td><code>{method} {path}</code></td>
								<td>{=if (body.is_empty()) Html.text("none") else <code>{body}</code>}</td>
								<td class:refused={status != 200}>{status.to_str()}</td>
								<td><code>{answer}</code></td>
							</tr>
						)}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The app asks them as functions. rocx writes <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> from the routes: a function
				for each endpoint, of its params (<code>{"{}"}</code> with none). On the server it has the handler; in
				the browser's copy it is the method and the URL, and nothing the handler does:
			</p>
			<Versus>
				<Code title="On the server" source={Sources.endpoint_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title="In the browser's copy" source={Sources.endpoint_api_browser.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				This component asks the other one from a handler, for the word clicked,
				<code>{"Endpoint.send!(Api.get_api_shout_word({ word: word }), {}, Call.fetch!)"}</code>, and shows the
				answer, or why there is none. On a copy of this site that is only files, these three words' answers
				are files, which the server wrote as the site was built:
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo inspect="shout" waiting="Nothing yet: shout a word.">
			<div data-demo="shout"><Shout /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				And this page's endpoint asked it as a function run there and then,
				<code>{"Endpoint.run!(endpoint, request, context, {})"}</code>, with no HTTP:
				<strong>{asked!({}).shouted}</strong>.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A page and endpoints at one path.</strong> A GET a browser sends (one that accepts HTML)
					is the page's; every other request is the endpoints'. A method nothing there answers is a 405,
					saying which are (<code>Allow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Behind middleware.</strong> This site's default middleware refuses a POST, PUT, PATCH or
					DELETE that a page of another site sends (its <code>Origin</code> isn't this server) with a 403;
					a client that isn't a page (<code>curl</code>) sends no
					<code>Origin</code>. A route file may name more (<code>middleware = Middlewares.member</code>):
					see <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Typed at both ends.</strong> The browser's copy can't hold the handler, so its types come
					from where the component uses it; the same component is built for the server too, against the
					handler, so a mismatch doesn't compile there.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<details>
				<summary>The component: <code>{Sources.demo_shout.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_shout.file} source={Sources.demo_shout.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="events">Events, as they happen</Heading>
			<p>
				An answer is sent once it is whole. For what the server learns a piece at a time (a job's
				progress, a feed, what others did), an endpoint can stream events instead, as it makes them
				(server-sent events). <code>events!</code> in a <code>+server.roc</code> answers a GET that asks for
				them (<code>accept: text/event-stream</code>) with <code>Events</code>: here, the counts from 1 to
				<code>params.to</code>, each with its square, 300 ms apart:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_count.file} source={Sources.endpoint_count.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A stream is a loop.</strong> <code>Events.stream</code> is given <code>send!</code>, which
					sends an event, and <code>sleep!</code>, which waits so many milliseconds; it sends, sleeps and goes
					round again, and the stream ends when it returns. The server runs it on a thread of its own, so
					its sleeps hold up no other request. When the page goes, <code>send!</code> says so
					(<code>Err(Gone)</code>), a sleep ends early, and <code>?</code> ends the loop.
					<code>Events.from_list(items, ms)</code> and <code>Events.map</code> make the simple ones.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Each event is JSON</strong>, of the type <code>events!</code> says, on the wire as a
					<code>data:</code> line; an <code>end</code> event says it is over, so a page doesn't ask again.
					Anything may listen: <code>curl -N -H 'accept: text/event-stream' localhost:8080/api/count/3</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A component listens</strong> from a handler, with <code>Endpoint.listen!</code>: each event
					runs the handler it is given, as a click would, with the event, of the endpoint's type
					(<code>Event(counted)</code>), then <code>Ended</code>, or <code>Failed</code> with why (a refusal
					is a <code>Refused</code>, with its status). Started in an
					<a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect</a>, it listens while the component is there (again
					when what the effect read changes) and stops when it goes. An event that says an endpoint's data
					changed can read it again: <code>refresh!(memo!)</code>, and the memo GETs its URL.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Behind middleware, as any endpoint.</strong> A guard refuses a stream as it refuses
					anything else, before the loop runs.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_count.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_count_update.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Its Start asks for a stream: each count is a row as the server sends it, and the component says
				when the server stopped sending (it ended the stream, and <code>Ended</code> came); Start again asks
				for a new one. It isn't live on this page, because a copy of this site as files can't stream: a host
				of files answers nothing with <code>text/event-stream</code>. With <code>just dev</code>, the
				<code>curl</code> above shows one.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The component: <code>{Sources.demo_count.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_count.file} source={Sources.demo_count.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="forms">Forms: actions and submissions</Heading>
			<p>
				A form posts to an endpoint as any form posts: the endpoint reads its fields as a record of strings
				(<code>{"{ name : Str }"}</code>), and answers. Solid Router calls that an action, and
				<code>useSubmission</code> says how the latest one went. Here the endpoint is the action: it has a
				URL, a method and types already. <code>use_submission!(endpoint)</code>, in a component, is its
				latest submission: whether it is on its way, what was typed, and the endpoint's result or its
				refusal.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="rocx"
				source={"import pf.Router exposing [use_submission!]\n\njoin = Api.post_members_join({})\nsubmission = use_submission!(join)\n<form method=\"post\" action={Endpoint.url_of(join)}>\n\t<input name=\"name\" value={submission.field!(\"name\")} />\n\t<button disabled?={submission.pending!()}>Join</button>\n\t<Show when!={submission.error!()}>{|problem!| <p class=\"problem\">{Server.explain(problem!())}</p>}</Show>\n</form>"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>With the runtime</strong>, a form posting to the endpoint of a submission a component reads
					is sent with <code>fetch</code>, and the page stays. <code>pending!()</code> holds until the answer
					is in; then <code>result!()</code> is the endpoint's result, or <code>error!()</code> its refusal
					(<code>Refused(409, "…")</code>), or why there was no answer. <code>input!()</code> is what was sent,
					as the endpoint reads it, and <code>field!(name)</code> one field. The latest submission is the one
					that counts; <code>clear!()</code>, in a handler, forgets it. A redirect of the endpoint's own
					(<code>Err(Answer(Server.redirect(…)))</code>, setting a cookie, say) sends the browser there.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Without it</strong> (before it loads, or with JavaScript off), the form posts, and a page of
					JSON or of text would come back. Instead the endpoint sends the browser back to the page the form
					was on, with a flash: a cookie of a minute holding where it posted, what was typed, and the result
					or the refusal. That page's submission is the flash, so it shows the refusal, with what was typed
					in its field, as it would have with the runtime, and the browser hydrates it as the server
					rendered it. The next page view lets it go. <code>{"Server.flash!({})"}</code> reads it as it came.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A redirect is the endpoint's to answer, either way.</strong> An endpoint whose forms should
					go somewhere when they succeed answers <code>Err(Answer(Server.redirect(…)))</code>, as the endpoint
					behind this site's <a href={Paths.members_join}>joining page</a> does (it sets a cookie too).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Where it differs from Solid:</strong> a record has no getters, so the fields are methods;
					the form's <code>action</code> is the endpoint's URL (<code>Endpoint.url_of</code>), since Roc can't
					turn an endpoint into a string by itself; <code>input!()</code> is the endpoint's own type, not a
					<code>FormData</code>. There is no <code>useSubmissions</code>, <code>useAction</code> or
					<code>.with</code> yet.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="build">What rocx build does</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>Names each endpoint</strong> after its method and its path, in
						<code>Generated/Api.roc</code>: <code>get_api_echo_message</code> for <code>get!</code> in
						<code>routes/api/echo/[message]/+server.roc</code>, a function of the path's params, so no URL is
						written twice and a link with the wrong params doesn't compile.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>Routes it.</strong> <code>Generated/Routes.roc</code> gives each path's requests to its
						page (a GET that accepts HTML) or to its endpoints, past the app's default middleware and the route
						file's own. The GETs the server makes as it renders go the same way.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>Leaves what it does out of the browser's copy.</strong> The browser's app is built from a
						copy of the site rocx writes (<code>--client site/build/client</code>), in which <code>Api</code>
						is each endpoint's method and URL and nothing else, and a route file has no
						<code>middleware</code> (only the server runs it, and it may read the environment or the
						database), as the <a href="#endpoints">endpoints</a> above show.
					</p>
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="server-functions">server!, deprecated</Heading>
			<p>
				Before a page could read an endpoint as it rendered, its data came from a server function,
				<code>{"name! = server!(|request, input| …)"}</code>, called at <code>POST /rocx/call/…</code>. A page
				with an endpoint too defined its data twice, and the server function's URL was one nobody chose.
				Server functions still work, and <code>rocx build</code> warns at each one; they will be removed.
			</p>
			<p>
				To move one, put its body in a <code>get!</code> in a route's <code>+server.roc</code>: the page's
				own, or a path of its own for a layout or a component that many pages show. Read its input from the
				path's params or the query, and what its middleware handed on from the handler's
				<code>context</code> instead of <code>Server.context!</code>. Then call
				<code>{"Endpoint.get!(Api.get_…(params))"}</code> where it was called. DECISIONS.md has the why
				(D-023).
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Limits, for now</Heading>
			<Callout kind="note" title="A working prototype">
				<p>The pieces are tested together, on the server and in a browser. Some are plainly missing.</p>
			</Callout>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A page reads with GET.</strong> While it renders, the server answers GETs of the app's own
					endpoints (a path, <code>/…</code>); anything else, a POST or another site's URL, is a handler's,
					sent from the browser.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A form the runtime sends is URL-encoded.</strong> A form with files
					(<code>multipart/form-data</code>) posts as the browser posts it, and an endpoint reads its body
					itself.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Its types are the browser's too.</strong> A handler may use what only the server has, but
					what it takes and answers must be types the browser's copy knows.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What goes and comes is JSON.</strong> An <code>F64</code> goes as a number, and one JSON
					can't write (NaN, an infinity) is a 500 that says so. The builtin <code>Try</code> has no JSON encoder
					or parser on this Roc, so a result can't hold one: write the tag union instead,
					<code>{"[Ok(Str), Err([NotFound])]"}</code>, which a <code>match</code> reads as it reads a
					<code>Try</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A static copy of the site has no server.</strong> Its pages are files, and so are the answers
					the server gave as it was built: each page's GETs, in the page, and the GETs its islands make from
					handlers (<code>just site</code> writes those). A read it didn't make then, a POST, a stream: those
					need the server, <code>just dev</code> or wherever the server's app runs. This site's demos need
					none: <a href="https://roc-solid.github.io/">roc-solid.github.io</a> is such a copy, files on GitHub
					Pages, and every demo works there.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Only what a handler does leaves the browser's copy.</strong> Anything else a component
					imports ships, as a component's code always does; and the server must check what a request asks
					for itself.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* Requests, and what the server answered. */
	.http td code {
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.http td:nth-child(2) {
		color: var(--text-faint);
	}

	.http td.refused {
		color: var(--bad);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.http td:last-child code {
		white-space: pre-wrap;
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}
</style>
